[the sign of the closed vector is not cosmetic]
\label{rem:wrongsign}
At $k=2$ the candidate $(1,+G_{12}/G_{22})$ differs from the minimiser of
Proposition~\ref{prop:K2} by $\delta=(0,2G_{12}/G_{22})$, and
\eqref{eq:pivot} prices it exactly:
$\delta^{\T}G_2\delta=4G_{12}^{2}/G_{22}$, so the relative excess is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:wrongsign}
  \rho=g_2\,\delta^{\T}G_2\delta
  =\frac{4G_{12}^{2}/G_{22}}{G_{11}\bigl(1-r_{12}^{2}\bigr)}
  =\frac{4r_{12}^{2}}{1-r_{12}^{2}}
  \qquad\text{identically},
\end{equation}
which diverges precisely where the cancellation is deep. The pivot identity
therefore also quantifies how a wrong closed vector fails, and it does so
without a single inequality.

