claims.source_body:
[bandwidth $\Rightarrow$ $\eta$-rank]
\label{lem:etarank}
Let $x_1,\dots,x_R$ and $y_1,\dots,y_C$ be real and let $I\subseteq\R$ contain
every $x_b+y_e$. Let $\Omega\ge0$ be the number of frequencies
$\nu_1,\dots,\nu_\Omega>0$, let $q\ge0$ be a degree bound, and let
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bandexp}
  W_\Omega(\tau)=p_0(\tau)+\sum_{j=1}^{\Omega}
  \bigl[p_j(\tau)\cos(\nu_j\tau)+\tilde p_j(\tau)\sin(\nu_j\tau)\bigr],
  \qquad \deg p_j,\deg\tilde p_j\le q .
\end{equation}
If $W:I\to\R$ satisfies $\sup_{\tau\in I}|W(\tau)-W_\Omega(\tau)|\le\eta$, then
the matrix $F_{be}=W(x_b+y_e)$ obeys
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:etarank}
  \sigma_{r+1}(F)\;\le\;\eta\sqrt{RC},
  \qquad r=(2\Omega+1)(q+1);
\end{equation}
if the expansion \eqref{eq:bandexp} has no polynomial term $p_0$, then
$r=2\Omega(q+1)$ suffices. Row and column rescalings
$F\mapsto\diag(\alpha)F\diag(\beta)$ leave $r$ unchanged and replace the
right-hand side by $\eta\lVert\alpha\rVert_2\lVert\beta\rVert_2$.

