claims.source_body:
[the three linear functionals are dual-point reads]
\label{lem:dualpoint}
Let $D$ be admissible, let $K=2$, and take the prime-power instance of
Section~\ref{sec:window}: $u_\ell=\log n_\ell$,
$\varrho_\ell=2\Lambda(n_\ell)/\sqrt{n_\ell}$ over the prime powers
$n_\ell\le X$, with $X$ the smaller of $e^{2\alpha}$ and the declared node cap,
and with every read interior, $1\le\tau_\ell=u_\ell/D\le M-1$. Write the band
representation of the polarised weight $w^{(ij)}$ of \eqref{eq:Yread}, which
exists by Lemma~\ref{lem:band}, as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bandij}
  W^{(ij)}(\tau)=\sum_{k=1,2}
  \bigl[(a_k+b_k\tau)\cos(\omega_k\tau)
  +(c_k+d_k\tau)\sin(\omega_k\tau)\bigr],
\end{equation}
its coefficients being those of Lemma~\ref{lem:spline} with the letters chosen
so as to leave $\gamma$ for the dual points, and let
$\Lambda_0^{(ij)}=\sum_k(|a_k|+|c_k|)+M\sum_k(|b_k|+|d_k|)$ and $\eta^{(ij)}$
be the coefficient budget and the interpolation bound of
\eqref{eq:splinebound} for that weight. Put
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:dualpoints}
  \gamma_k=\frac{\omega_k}D=\frac{2\pi k}{ND}\quad(k=1,2),
  \qquad
  \Delta\gamma=\gamma_2-\gamma_1=\frac{2\pi}{ND},
  \qquad
  T_q(\gamma)=\sum_{n\le X}\varrho_n(\log n)^{q}n^{\mathrm i\gamma} .
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:dualread}
  S_{ij}=\sum_{k=1,2}\operatorname{Re}
  \Bigl[(a_k-\mathrm ic_k)T_0(\gamma_k)
  +\tfrac1D(b_k-\mathrm id_k)T_1(\gamma_k)\Bigr]+E_{ij},
  \qquad
  |E_{ij}|\le\eta^{(ij)}\lVert\varrho\rVert_1 ,
\end{equation}
so each of the three reads is a value of one exponential sum of length $X$, and
of its first logarithmic moment, at the two points \eqref{eq:dualpoints} and at
no others. If in addition the Montgomery--Vaughan inequality
\cite{MontgomeryVaughan1974} is applied at those two points, in the form
$\sum_{k\le2}|T_0(\gamma_k)|^{2}
\le(X+\Delta\gamma^{-1})\lVert\varrho\rVert_2^{2}$ and the same with the weights
$\varrho_n\log n$ for $T_1$, then
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mvread}
  |S_{ij}|\;\le\;\Lambda_0^{(ij)}\,Q+\eta^{(ij)}\lVert\varrho\rVert_1,
  \qquad
  Q^{2}=\bigl(X+\Delta\gamma^{-1}\bigr)\lVert\varrho\rVert_2^{2} .
\end{equation}

